1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,840
In 729, a prince, his wife, and his sons

2
00:00:02,840 --> 00:00:05,280
all took their own lives on the same day.

3
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:09,390
Eight years later, the four brothers who had driven them to it were dead,

4
00:00:09,390 --> 00:00:12,910
all from the same disease, all in the same year.

5
00:00:12,910 --> 00:00:15,440
People began to whisper that it was a curse.

6
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:18,190
Today we’re in the Nara period.

7
00:00:18,190 --> 00:00:22,950
The Nara period begins in 710, when the capital moved to Heijo-kyo,

8
00:00:22,950 --> 00:00:27,760
and it ends in 794, when the capital moved to Heian-kyo.

9
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:30,940
The capital’s lifespan: just eighty-four years.

10
00:00:30,940 --> 00:00:35,510
And in that time, the capital kept moving house again and again.

11
00:00:35,510 --> 00:00:37,510
Let me give you the answer first.

12
00:00:37,510 --> 00:00:41,450
The capital never settled down because the country was hit by three shocks,

13
00:00:41,450 --> 00:00:44,780
over and over: feuds between powerful families,

14
00:00:44,780 --> 00:00:49,580
epidemics and disasters, and the problem of who would inherit the throne.

15
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:53,010
Today, we’ll read the Nara period through those three shocks.

16
00:00:54,010 --> 00:00:57,120
First, let’s look at the country before the shaking began.

17
00:00:57,120 --> 00:01:00,650
With its capital at Heijo-kyo, today’s city of Nara,

18
00:01:00,650 --> 00:01:04,480
Japan began building a full-scale state run by written law.

19
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:08,250
At the center of that effort was Fujiwara no Fuhito.

20
00:01:08,250 --> 00:01:11,780
Fuhito’s method was to marry his daughters to emperors.

21
00:01:11,780 --> 00:01:16,410
If a grandson became emperor, Fuhito would be the emperor’s grandfather.

22
00:01:16,410 --> 00:01:18,830
He pulled this off across two generations,

23
00:01:18,830 --> 00:01:23,110
and the Fujiwara family became tightly bound to the imperial family.

24
00:01:23,110 --> 00:01:25,480
Have you heard of the Habsburgs in Europe?

25
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:27,490
They were the family that expanded their lands

26
00:01:27,490 --> 00:01:29,720
not through war, but through marriage.

27
00:01:29,720 --> 00:01:31,830
Fuhito’s thinking was close to that.

28
00:01:31,830 --> 00:01:34,990
He took power not with the sword, but with the wedding.

29
00:01:35,990 --> 00:01:37,140
The first shock.

30
00:01:37,140 --> 00:01:41,320
But once Fuhito died, his blueprint started to shake almost at once.

31
00:01:41,320 --> 00:01:44,930
The man who stepped into the center of government was Prince Nagaya,

32
00:01:44,930 --> 00:01:46,880
a member of the imperial family.

33
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:51,300
Meanwhile, Fuhito’s four sons wanted a government run not by the emperor,

34
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:53,230
but by the Fujiwara family.

35
00:01:53,230 --> 00:01:58,030
In 729, Prince Nagaya was accused of plotting against the state,

36
00:01:58,030 --> 00:01:59,810
and he took his own life.

37
00:01:59,810 --> 00:02:01,940
This is the Nagaya Incident.

38
00:02:01,940 --> 00:02:05,850
Real power passed into the hands of the four Fujiwara brothers.

39
00:02:05,850 --> 00:02:07,970
But the seesaw soon tipped the other way.

40
00:02:07,970 --> 00:02:10,220
The next to take power was Tachibana

41
00:02:10,220 --> 00:02:13,510
no Moroe, who came from the imperial family.

42
00:02:13,510 --> 00:02:18,280
A young Fujiwara, Fujiwara no Hirotsugu, resented this.

43
00:02:18,280 --> 00:02:22,760
He was sent away to Kyushu, and in 740, he raised an army.

44
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:26,020
This is the Rebellion of Fujiwara no Hirotsugu.

45
00:02:26,020 --> 00:02:30,150
It was put down quickly, but the mood in the capital turned uneasy overnight.

46
00:02:31,150 --> 00:02:32,400
The second shock.

47
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:36,410
What ended the four Fujiwara brothers’ rule wasn’t an enemy.

48
00:02:36,410 --> 00:02:39,340
It was a massive epidemic in 737.

49
00:02:39,340 --> 00:02:42,460
All four brothers died of the disease in the same year.

50
00:02:42,460 --> 00:02:46,060
From there, the fear of the people in the capital kept growing.

51
00:02:46,060 --> 00:02:48,500
And in the middle of Hirotsugu’s rebellion,

52
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:52,170
Emperor Shomu suddenly left the capital and headed east.

53
00:02:52,170 --> 00:02:54,490
For the next five years, the capital wandered.

54
00:02:54,490 --> 00:02:57,500
Kuni-kyo, Naniwa-no-miya, Shigaraki-no-miya.

55
00:02:57,500 --> 00:03:00,530
The place Tachibana no Moroe backed and the place

56
00:03:00,530 --> 00:03:04,370
backed by the rising Fujiwara no Nakamaro didn’t match,

57
00:03:04,370 --> 00:03:06,540
so the capital never stayed put.

58
00:03:06,540 --> 00:03:10,280
To calm the fear, Emperor Shomu turned to Buddhism.

59
00:03:10,280 --> 00:03:15,160
In 743, he announced that he would build a gigantic statue of the Buddha.

60
00:03:15,160 --> 00:03:18,540
But the people doing the actual work were ordinary people.

61
00:03:18,540 --> 00:03:22,780
Under that heavy burden, their anger grew, fires broke out one after another,

62
00:03:22,780 --> 00:03:24,380
and earthquakes kept coming.

63
00:03:24,380 --> 00:03:27,270
Emperor Shomu took it to mean that even heaven was angry,

64
00:03:27,270 --> 00:03:31,380
and in 745, he moved the capital back to Heijo-kyo.

65
00:03:31,380 --> 00:03:34,380
The Great Buddha was completed in 752.

66
00:03:34,380 --> 00:03:36,760
This is the high point of the Nara period.

67
00:03:36,760 --> 00:03:39,660
That Great Buddha isn’t a symbol of a wealthy nation.

68
00:03:39,660 --> 00:03:42,010
It’s the shape of a cornered nation’s prayer.

69
00:03:43,010 --> 00:03:44,340
The third shock.

70
00:03:44,340 --> 00:03:48,430
Emperor Shomu was succeeded by his daughter, Empress Koken.

71
00:03:48,430 --> 00:03:51,550
But the one who actually ran the government was her mother,

72
00:03:51,550 --> 00:03:54,940
Empress Dowager Komyo, Fuhito’s daughter.

73
00:03:54,940 --> 00:03:58,420
The Empress Dowager relied heavily on Fujiwara no

74
00:03:58,420 --> 00:04:02,070
Nakamaro, and Empress Koken, without real power,

75
00:04:02,070 --> 00:04:04,350
handed the throne to Emperor Junnin.

76
00:04:04,350 --> 00:04:07,460
Emperor Junnin had been raised in Nakamaro’s household.

77
00:04:07,460 --> 00:04:09,450
When the Empress Dowager died,

78
00:04:09,450 --> 00:04:12,990
the retired Empress Koken was finally free to act.

79
00:04:12,990 --> 00:04:15,590
At her side was a monk named Dokyo,

80
00:04:15,590 --> 00:04:18,410
who had won her trust by nursing her through an illness.

81
00:04:18,410 --> 00:04:20,260
Think of Rasputin, the man

82
00:04:20,260 --> 00:04:23,870
who worked his way into the family of Russia’s last tsar.

83
00:04:23,870 --> 00:04:27,680
A holy man who enters the heart of the court through a sickbed.

84
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:29,060
It’s that same picture.

85
00:04:29,060 --> 00:04:35,890
In 764, the two camps clashed in battle, and Nakamaro was defeated and killed.

86
00:04:35,890 --> 00:04:39,130
Emperor Junnin was exiled to Awaji Island,

87
00:04:39,130 --> 00:04:43,920
and the retired empress took the throne a second time, as Empress Shotoku.

88
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:47,440
Empress Shotoku tried to make Dokyo the next emperor.

89
00:04:47,440 --> 00:04:50,810
A monk, neither royal nor noble, on the throne.

90
00:04:50,810 --> 00:04:53,480
The nobles banded together and stopped it.

91
00:04:53,480 --> 00:04:55,100
That was in 769.

92
00:04:56,100 --> 00:04:57,380
The final move.

93
00:04:57,380 --> 00:05:01,510
In 770, Empress Shotoku died without naming an heir,

94
00:05:01,510 --> 00:05:05,160
and the nobles hurried to put Emperor Konin on the throne.

95
00:05:05,160 --> 00:05:09,050
Here, the imperial line switched from the bloodline of Emperor Tenmu

96
00:05:09,050 --> 00:05:11,370
to the bloodline of Emperor Tenji,

97
00:05:11,370 --> 00:05:13,760
the two brothers from our last episode.

98
00:05:13,760 --> 00:05:17,090
Emperor Konin’s son, Emperor Kanmu, thought it over.

99
00:05:17,090 --> 00:05:19,770
Heijo-kyo was the capital of the old bloodline.

100
00:05:19,770 --> 00:05:21,780
A new bloodline needs a new capital.

101
00:05:21,780 --> 00:05:25,910
In 784, the capital moved to Nagaoka-kyo.

102
00:05:25,910 --> 00:05:29,130
But people close to the emperor died one after another,

103
00:05:29,130 --> 00:05:33,890
diviners blamed vengeful spirits, and the rivers flooded on top of it all.

104
00:05:33,890 --> 00:05:37,080
Emperor Kanmu gave up on Nagaoka-kyo too,

105
00:05:37,080 --> 00:05:42,190
and in 794, he moved the capital to Heian-kyo, today’s Kyoto.

106
00:05:42,190 --> 00:05:45,370
And with that, the Nara period came to a close.

107
00:05:46,370 --> 00:05:50,390
Family feuds, epidemics and disasters, and the question of an heir.

108
00:05:50,390 --> 00:05:52,580
Pushed along by these three shocks,

109
00:05:52,580 --> 00:05:57,520
the capital kept moving, and after eighty-four years, it left Nara behind.

110
00:05:57,520 --> 00:05:59,110
Here’s a question for you.

111
00:05:59,110 --> 00:06:02,700
If you were Emperor Shomu, would you have built the Great Buddha,

112
00:06:02,700 --> 00:06:05,040
or spent that wealth feeding your people?

113
00:06:05,040 --> 00:06:06,530
Tell me in the comments.

114
00:06:06,530 --> 00:06:09,420
If you enjoyed this, give it a like and subscribe,

115
00:06:09,420 --> 00:06:11,390
so you don’t miss the next episode.

116
00:06:11,390 --> 00:06:13,180
Next time: the Heian period.

117
00:06:13,180 --> 00:06:16,210
This time, the capital lasts about four hundred years.

118
00:06:16,210 --> 00:06:17,550
Let’s see what changed.

